Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Third Echo Reaches the Floor Drain
- Story & Context: The debut begins at 118 BPM with one short Dorian chord stab and a three-quarter-note delay. Aino starts a semitone below the chord’s fifth and slowly bends upward, reaching exact pitch when the third echo arrives. Noor confirms the landing with one deadened piano octave. The process repeats with a different target note while Mikael keeps the kick unchanged. Oskar strikes the steel plate only after the fifth cycle, allowing its metallic decay to overlap the fading electronic repeats. Midway through the track, the delay feedback is shortened so Aino has less time to reach each target; her glides become steeper without the tempo changing. The final phrase misses its target deliberately and fades below pitch while a room microphone captures water dripping into an old floor drain.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty white-tiled refrigeration room with a floor drain in the foreground, three faint concentric echo rings drawn across condensation on glass, a polished musical saw curving toward the drain, prepared-piano key and copper pipe in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “third echo,” “reach the drain,” “hold below,” and “miss the last one.”

Track 2: Valve Twelve Forgets the Original Chord
- Story & Context: Mikael programs the driest track on the EP at 122 BPM. Each chord stab is heard only once at full brightness; subsequent repeats are filtered until the listener remembers the harmony more than hears it. Aino plays short descending saw curves that begin on exact piano coordinates but drift away as the echoes darken. Elias enters every second bar with one foam-muted root note, then gradually stops playing as the delay field becomes sufficient to imply the tonal center. Oskar rubs the edge of the suspended steel plate with a rubber mallet, producing a low groan that is gated to appear only between kicks. In the final minute, the dry source chord is removed entirely and only the existing delay loop remains. Aino continues to phrase against it until the feedback collapses into noise.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old refrigeration valve labeled 12 beside a fading technical diagram, concentric waveform traces becoming thinner and darker with each repetition, musical saw edge reflected in brushed steel. Spoken fragments limited to “valve twelve,” “forget the source,” “follow the repeat,” and “only the tail remains.”

Track 3: Defrost Timer Stops Before the Pitch Arrives
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 114 BPM and begins with a visible mechanical timer set to ninety seconds. Aino is given one long upward saw bend intended to reach the tonic exactly when the timer reaches zero. Mikael gradually lengthens the delay time by tiny increments, making the echo grid drift against the kick while Noor keeps striking the same prepared-piano fifth every eight bars. The listener can hear two clocks: the fixed dance pulse and the moving delay network. Oskar bows the steel plate near its edge, creating a narrow beating tone that approaches Aino’s pitch from above. Ten seconds before zero, every drum except the kick disappears. The timer unexpectedly stops mechanically at three seconds. Aino continues bending but never completes the arrival; she releases the bow and lets the steel fall silent first. The last sound is the stopped timer’s faint motor hum.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Mechanical defrost timer frozen at three seconds inside a cream control panel, musical saw bow suspended just above the blade, narrow condensation trail running downward, one prepared-piano fifth marked on maintenance paper. Spoken fragments limited to “three seconds,” “timer stopped,” “pitch still moving,” and “do not force arrival.”

